\description{
Every result prints with \code{print()}, and so does its \code{summary()}. \code{print()}
shows a result's main evidence and any flags and ends with a pointer to
where to look next; \code{summary()} shows the full evidence, with the reason
for every flag. Printing changes nothing in the object.
}
\details{
\strong{Decimals.} Each statistic prints with the decimals of its kind: three
for a \emph{p} value and for a fit index such as CFI or RMSEA, and two for most
others, such as a correlation, a reliability, or a loading. A few tables
give a quantity three decimals where values often agree to two, such as
residual correlations. A number given as \code{digits} replaces these decimals
for every statistic shown, references and interval bounds included. It
leaves alone:
\itemize{
\item \emph{p} values, degrees of freedom, significance levels, counts, and
percentages, which keep their own format;
\item the extra decimals a value takes when it would otherwise print as the
reference it is flagged against, so a flag never reads ".40 is below
.40";
\item a bound of a predicted region, which never shows fewer decimals than it
was given;
\item text written when the analysis ran, such as a flag's explanation from the
decision log, which keeps the decimals it was written with.
}

Rounding is for display only, half away from zero. Returned values are not
rounded.

Some printouts show no statistic for \code{digits} to change: a \code{nomo_model} and
a \code{nomo_partial} print syntax, \code{print()} of a \code{nomo_screen} shows counts,
the summary of a \code{nomo_run} shows what its stages recorded, and the cells of
a \code{nomo_apa_table} are formatted by APA rules when the table is built. Given
\code{digits}, these say so in a message and print as usual.

The results of \code{\link[=nomo_screen]{nomo_screen()}} and \code{\link[=nomo_factors]{nomo_factors()}} also have print pages
of their own: \code{\link[=print.nomo_screen]{print.nomo_screen()}}, \code{\link[=print.summary_nomo_screen]{print.summary_nomo_screen()}},
